# 07 — WHY MUSHROOM IS SO IMPORTANT

This is one place where the compatibility engine becomes more interesting than recipe retrieval.

The bean already possesses an earthy character.

You might initially assume:

> Earthy + earthy = bad.

But that's incomplete.

There are different dimensions of "earthiness."

The bean contributes:

* starch-associated aroma
* vegetal/earthy compounds
* mild savory character

Mushrooms contribute:

* glutamate
* guanylate
* volatile mushroom compounds
* roasted/forest-like aromatic complexity

So instead of simply increasing the same signal:

[
E_{bean}+E_{mushroom}
]

you get:

[
\boxed{
\text{Bean savory}+
\text{mushroom umami}+
\text{lipid aroma}+
\text{acid lift}
}
]

The lemon then prevents the entire system from collapsing into a heavy earthy mass.

---

# 08 — BROWN BUTTER IS AN OPERATOR

Don't treat butter merely as an ingredient.

Treat it as a **chemical processing medium**.

Heating butter produces:

* water removal
* milk-solid browning
* Maillard-derived compounds
* lipid-soluble aromatic compounds

Then:

[
\Phi_{\text{brown-butter}}
(\text{bean})
]

changes the sensory state of the bean without fundamentally changing its structure.

The fat also acts as a carrier for hydrophobic aromatic molecules.

Therefore:

[
\text{Brown butter}
\rightarrow
\text{aroma extraction}
+
\text{mouthfeel modification}
+
\text{thermal complexity}
]

---

# 09 — ACID SHOULD COME LATE

This is particularly important for the system you're designing.

Don't indiscriminately acidify the bean during its primary cooking stage.

Instead:

[
q_{base}
\xrightarrow{\text{fat/aromatic extraction}}
q_1
\xrightarrow{\text{umami integration}}
q_2
\xrightarrow{\text{acid finishing}}
q_3
]

The acid becomes a **finishing-state operator**.

Its purpose isn't simply "make it sour."

It changes perceived balance.

Too little:

> heavy / flat / starchy

Too much:

> sharp / thin / potentially texturally compromised

Optimal:

> creamy → savory → bright finish

