# 13 — THE REAL DISCOVERY ENGINE

The system should search for **complementarity**, not simply compatibility.

Define:

[
\boxed{
\mathcal{S}(I)=
\alpha C+
\beta K+
\gamma \Delta T+
\delta \Delta A+
\epsilon B-
\lambda D
}
]

where:

* (C) = chemical compatibility
* (K) = flavor compatibility
* (\Delta T) = textural contrast
* (\Delta A) = aromatic contrast/complementarity
* (B) = balance improvement
* (D) = dominance/conflict

Then search not just individual ingredients, but **sets**:

[
\boxed{
I^* =
\arg\max_I
\left[
Q(I)
+
\lambda V(I)
------------

\mu C(I)
\right]
}
]

where:

* (Q) = predicted sensory quality
* (V) = novelty
* (C) = complexity/conflict

This prevents the AI from producing:

> bean + garlic + onion + cumin + chili + paprika + tomato + cilantro + lime + cheese + bacon + five spices...

That's not optimization.

That's **feature accumulation**.

The engine should instead seek the **minimum number of ingredients capable of producing the maximum sensory dimensionality.**

